50
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.159
should be, you know, protected. There are a few basics like, hey,

51
00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:29.990
make sure you set up your database in the EU, ours is in Frankfurt,

52
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.159
and then make sure that, you know, only a few people have access to

53
00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:36.879
the sensitive stuff, with two-factor authentication and all the

54
00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:38.959
security protocols in place.

55
00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:44.230
In terms of the actual correctness of the data,

56
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.720
I actually don't worry too much about it

57
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:55.360
because we are not a data-driven company. In our case

58
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.680
we don't have millions and millions of data units

59
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.239
to build some models, to train models on our data.

60
00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:05.439
For us, what's important is that the correct clients get

