80
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.639
Okay. Well, it looks like we are out of time for questions.

81
00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:18.240
So, thank you to the panelists.

82
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.440
If we didn't get to your questions, feel free to ambush the

83
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.840
panelists outside and ask them.

84
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.029
And we'll go on to summarize

85
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:34.630
this track. So in summary, after everything that we've talked about,

86
00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:39.759
the recommendation before you build anything is to try to answer these

87
00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:43.520
10 questions that you see, and if you can't answer these questions, if

88
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.120
you want to actually implement AI, these are the places that you want

89
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.520
to start. Number one: is this problem worth solving, and does

90
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.800
leadership even care? That's going to be very important to get buy-in

91
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.880
from leadership. Is the outcome measurable, and can I define success

92
00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.160
in one sentence? Is the data accessible?

93
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.000
Is it trustworthy? Do you have the data that you can use in order to

94
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.319
solve the problem? Is AI even the right tool, or would a

95
00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:12.550
rule or a dashboard do the job?

96
00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.990
Don't overcomplicate things and go for the most simple solution.

97
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:19.670
Do I have a named sponsor who will champion this?

98
00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.360
Somebody that you can always go to, that will socialize it in the

99
00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.510
organization, that will help with adoption.

100
00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.880
Is the output embedded where users actually work? And that is starred

101
00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.600
because maybe the workflow needs to change.

102
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.240
Maybe the way that they work today isn't the best way.

103
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.070
And this is an opportunity to take a step back in an organization

104
00:06:40.479 --> 00:06:44.790
and figure out a better workflow that will integrate AI into it.

105
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.800
It's important to ask what is my baseline and how will I measure

106
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.670
improvement. How do I know that it's going to make an impact?

107
00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.029
Who's going to be responsible for tracking this value after go-live?

108
00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.720
Ask, can I explain the result in three sentences to a non-technical

109
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.880
leader? I always like to have an elevator pitch ready, so that if I have

110
00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.630
one minute to explain to somebody, especially in leadership,

111
00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:14.479
what the purpose of the project is and what the impact is,

112
00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:18.319
I can do that. And have I socialized this with the right

113
00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:20.319
stakeholders after the demo?

114
00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:23.759
The change management, like we said, is this very important piece of

115
00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:27.680
it. How do people get to know about it and are convinced to start

116
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.919
using it and create a sea change in the organization where everybody

117
00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:34.880
starts using it? So those are the lessons.

